The measurement starts with the descriptions of the SM Higgs boson production which are obtained using the 
{\sc powheg V2}  generator for the five main production modes (ggH, VBF, WH, ZH and ttH) and are called signal samples. 
%as described in section \ref{chapter1_sec3} 
%~\cite{Bagnaschi:2011tu}, ~\cite{Nason:2009ai}, \cite{Hartanto:2015uka}, ~\cite{Luisoni:2013kna}.
WH and $Z$H production modes are simulated by the {\sc MiNLO HVJ} extension of the {\sc powheg}~\cite{Luisoni:2013kna}. 
Higgs decay to four lepton is simulated by {\sc JHUgen} generator~\cite{Gao:2010qx}. For the WH, ZH and ttH modes, Higgs is allowed
to decay to H$\to ZZ \to 2\ell2$X such that two leptons from 4-lepton events are produced from 
the decay of associated W, Z bosons or top quarks respectively. 
Showering of parton-level events is done using {\sc pythia8.209}, and in all cases matching is performed by  
allowing QCD emissions at all energies in the shower and vetoing them afterwards according to the 
{\sc powheg} internal scale. All samples are generated with the default NNPDF 3.1 NLO parton distribution 
functions (PDFs)~\cite{Ball:2014uwa}. The list of signal samples with the corresponding cross sections are shown in ~\cite{CMS:2019chr}.
These samples, for each production mode, are also used to assess model dependence of measured results as shown in the Table ~\ref{tab:efficiencySM}. The details of the method used to assess the model dependence will be 
described in Section \ref{sec:Systematics}.
The {\sc powheg} and {\sc NNLOPS} based samples for $\ggH$ production are utilized as theoretical predictions based on SM calculations for the comparison with measured results.\\
\par For the estimations of the backgrounds, {\sc powheg V2} interfaced with {\sc pythia8} was used to generate the dominant process $\Pq\Paq \to \Z \Z \to 4\ell$  at the NLO accuracy level. The $\Pg\Pg \! \to \! ZZ$ process is simulated at leading order (LO) with {\sc MCFM}. Further details on simulation and data samples are given in the sections \ref{sec:Datasets} and ~\ref{sec:Simulations}.
\par The measurements on Higgs cross sections (Inclusive and differential) are made in a fiducial region that matches the reconstruction level selection.
For Higgs hunting, firstly four-lepton candidates are built from the selected leptons, that pass the ${\rm SIP_{3D}} < 4$~ (${\rm SIP_{3D}}$ is significance of impact parameter obtained by dividing the impact parameter with associated uncertainty) vertex constraint and the isolation cuts where FSR photons are subtracted. {\bf Z candidates} are built from oppositely charged and same flavoured leptons ($e^+ e^-$, \, $\mu^+\mu^-$) with additional requirement on dilepton mass as $12 < m_{\ell\ell(\gamma)} < 120 GeV$. {\bf ZZ candidates} are then built from non-overlapping Z boson candidates. The one who is reconstructed with $m_{\ell\ell}$ closer to the SM Z boson mass is regarded as ${\rm Z_1}$ and other as ${\rm Z_2}$. {\bf Signal region} is built from the events containing one selected ZZ candidate at least. More details are given in section \ref{sec:events_selection}.


\par Integrated and differential fiducial cross section for $pp\to$H$\to4\ell$ are measured by performing a maximum likelihood fit of the signal and background parameterisations to the observed $4\ell$ mass distribution, $N_{\mathrm{obs}}(m_{4\ell})$, and the fiducial cross section ($\sigma_{\mathrm{fid}}$) is directly extracted from the fit. The systematic uncertainties are included in the form of nuisance parameters and are effectively integrated out in the fit procedure. The results are obtained using an asymptotic approach~\cite{LHC-HCG} with a test statistic based on the profile likelihood ratio~\cite{Cowan:2010js}. This procedure for the unfolding of the detector effects from the observed distributions is the same as in Refs.~\cite{CMSH4lFiducial8TeV}~and~\cite{CMSHggFiducial8TeV}. In the case of the differential cross section measurements,  the finite efficiencies and resolution effects are encoded in a detector response matrix which describes how events migrate from a given observable bin at the fiducial level to a given bin at the reconstruction level. This matrix is diagonally dominant, with sizeable off-diagonal elements for observables involving jets.
\par The dominant sources of systematic uncertainty are the experimental uncertainties in the lepton identification efficiencies and
luminosity measurement, and the theoretical sources of uncertainty are found to be subdominant.
In order to asses the model dependence of the measurement, the unfolding procedure is repeated
using different response matrices created by varying the relative fraction of each SM production mode
within its experimental constraints. The uncertainty is determined
to be negligible with respect to the experimental systematic uncertainties.	
\section{Results and outlook}	
	\par In order to make all the measurements independent of how Higgs is produced, all the cross sections are extracted within a fiducial phase space defined by the selections on lepton kinematics and event topology. The integrated fiducial cross sections are measured to be $2.73^{+0.30}_{-0.29}=2.73^{+0.23}_{-0.22}({\rm stat.})^{+0.24}_{-0.19}({\rm syst.})~{\rm fb}$ at 13\TeV.
The differential fiducial cross sections are presented as a function of %several kinematic observables which are sensitive to the Higgs-boson 
%production mechanisms: 
	rapidity and transverse momentum of the four-lepton system, associated jet multiplicity and transverse momentum of the leading jet.
The differential measurements are done in bins of the observables. For each kinematic or differential observable, selection procedure of the binning boundaries has been optimized in order to have a similar expected measured cross-section for every bin of the observable. For the differential cross section measurements, the background is to be subtracted at the reconstruction level for each bin
of the observable. The mass spectrum for the background is not same for all differential bins because it does not have a resonance structure. For the irreducible and reducible backgrounds, the four-lepton mass spectra are obtained as the $m_{4\ell}$ template shapes through simulated events and the control regions in the data respectively for each bin of the observable for differential measurements as the procedure is described in Ref.~\cite{Chatrchyan:2013mxa}.
%	These measurements are important to test the Standard Model predictions in the Higgs sector and can be used to constrain phase spaces of many theories beyond the Standard Model.
\par
	These measurements are important to test the Standard Model predictions in the Higgs sector and can be used to constrain phase spaces of many theories beyond the Standard Model.  All the integrated and differential measurements are compared with the theoretical predictions based on the Standard Model and found to be consistent within their uncertainties. More precise Higgs boson properties measurements are needed to spot any possible deviation
from SM predictions which can be a doorway to new physics.

There are certain things that can be done which are beyond the scope of this dissertation. With more data, we can get more precise results. With full Run 2 data, more differential observables can be studied. Current measurements are based on {\sc powheg} and {\sc nnlops} prediction. However, additional theoretical prediction(s) for comparison with the observed cross section in differential bins can be used. Further, potential theory interpretations for differential observables can be studied.
